'''Four players''',
'''All pieces move as in normal chess. On the infinite board, however, rook, bishop and queen get very far: they may move up to ten squares beyond the outermost pieces – the game shows every reachable square.''',
'''A queen in open space: green dots are free squares, red rings are captures.''',
'''Castling and en passant''',
'''Castling squares are marked in blue. On the open board the king can also escape backwards.''',
'''<b>Castling:</b> King and rook must not have moved yet, the squares between them are empty, the king is not in check and does not pass over an attacked square. The king moves two squares towards the rook, the rook jumps over it.''',
'''<b>En passant:</b> If a pawn moves two squares, an enemy pawn may capture it in passing – with several players until the player who made the double step is to move again.''',
'''Promotion''',
'''With “start zone” the blue pawn becomes a queen as soon as it enters the yellow start zone.''',
'''<b>With two players:</b> as usual on the opponent’s back rank.''',
'''<b>With three or four players the host decides:</b> The pawn becomes a queen as soon as it enters <b>another player’s start zone</b> – or only when it reaches <b>the admirals’ back rank</b> of another player. Opposite, the extended back rank always counts too.''',
'''Whoever is in check must get out of it. No move may leave your own king in check. A king in check glows red.''',
